# Workflow

How work moves through this repository, and why. The step-by-step procedures are skills (`.agents/skills/`, indexed in [AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md)); this file holds the conventions and the reasons behind them.

## The two gates

Work is split so that a human decides twice:

1. **Plan gate.** The plan is posted on the issue and nothing is edited until the human approves it. A wrong direction is cheap to correct in a comment and expensive in a diff.
2. **Merge gate.** The agent opens a PR and stops. The human reviews and merges.

The gates are also the points where the human can switch models or hand the PR to a separate reviewing agent.

### Small changes

A typo, a dependency bump, a one-line fix: two waits are one too many. A change is **small** when it changes no behaviour beyond what the issue states, adds no dependency, changes no interface, schema or stored format, and needs no ADR. A small change skips the plan gate: the issue body is the plan, the agent implements and opens the PR, and the merge gate stays. The PR body says `No plan — small change` so the reviewer sees which lane was used.

If any of the conditions is in doubt, it is not small. The human's word on the issue overrides the definition in both directions: "plan first" forces a plan, "no plan needed" waives it.

### Enforcement

The merge gate should not depend on the agent's cooperation. Two things on {% if platform == 'github' %}GitHub{% else %}Forgejo{% endif %} make it hold; both are set up once per repository by its admin and are listed in the steps copier prints after generating the project.

- **Branch protection on `main`:** no direct pushes, merging only with an approving review.
{%- if platform == 'github' %} `just forge-protect` applies it. (GitHub offers branch protection on private repositories only on paid plans.)
{%- else %} fj cannot manage branch protection, so it is set by hand: repository **Settings → Branches → Add new rule**, branch name pattern `main`, disable push, required approvals `1`.
{%- endif %}
- **Agents use their own account or token without merge rights:** write access to branches and PRs, no admin rights. Protection binds non-admin accounts; a repository admin can bypass or remove it. An agent that runs with an admin's token is therefore not constrained by it — the gate only holds against a separate, non-admin account. A human who works alone merges their own PRs as admin, since nobody can approve their own PR.
- **Backstops in the repo:** {% if hooks != 'none' %}the `no-commit-to-main` pre-commit hook blocks local commits on `main`, and {% endif %}CI runs `just check` on every push and pull request.

There is no merge recipe. The plan gate stays a convention; the `plan-issue` skill carries it out.
{%- if agents %}

### Agent adapters

`AGENTS.md` and `.agents/skills/` are the tool-neutral core. On top of them this project has thin adapters for {% if 'claude' in agents %}Claude Code (`CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/`){% endif %}{% if 'claude' in agents and 'codex' in agents %} and {% endif %}{% if 'codex' in agents %}Codex (`.codex/`, loaded once the project is trusted in Codex){% endif %}. An adapter does three things and contains no logic of its own: it makes the skills discoverable, refuses the obvious commands for merging a PR or pushing to `main`, and runs `just stop-check` when the agent stops — {% if python == 'none' %}the project's `static` recipe, if it has one and{% else %}lint and typecheck if{% endif %} the working tree has changes, with failures handed back to the agent.

The refused commands are matched by their text, so they stop a slip, not a determined agent. The gate is the branch protection above.
{%- endif %}

### Second-agent review

`just review N` hands PR N to a second agent with the `review-pr` skill and posts what it prints as a PR comment. The reviewer is the command in `AGENT_REVIEWER` — anything that reads a prompt on stdin and prints an answer{% if agents %}; without it, `{% if 'claude' in agents %}claude -p{% else %}codex exec -{% endif %}` is used{% endif %}. A reviewer on a different model than the author finds more. `just mddiff` shows a word-level diff of the Markdown files a branch changes, which reads better than a line diff for prose.

## Where information lives

| Place | What belongs there |
|---|---|
| `docs/` | What should outlive the task: architecture, domain rules, conventions, runbooks, ADRs |
| Issue | The problem, the plan, decisions made along the way |
| PR body | The handoff: what changed, how it was verified, assumptions, open questions, ADRs |
| `.ai/` | Gitignored scratch: notes and scripts, possibly kept by the human across sessions. Body files for `just` go in `.ai/forge/`. Never referenced from committed files, never the only copy of something that matters |

Issues and PRs on {% if platform == 'github' %}GitHub{% else %}Forgejo{% endif %} are the authoritative record of planned and completed work, so a new session, another machine or another tool can pick up from them.
{%- if platform == 'both' %} GitHub is a mirror: the code is readable there, but issues, PRs and releases live on Forgejo.
{%- endif %}

When you find a bug, open an issue before or alongside the fix. Nothing gets fixed without a record. Decisions and findings do not stay in issues: they go into `docs/` or an ADR, and the issue points there when it closes.

**Drift policy.** If a change makes a doc wrong, fix the doc in the same PR. Move only stable conclusions into `docs/`; task chatter stays in the issue. Put a finding into the existing file it belongs to; create a new `docs/domain/` file only for a subject that has no home. Write an ADR for every non-obvious architectural decision.

## Conventions

**Branches:** `<type>/<short-description>`, always from an up-to-date `main`. Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `chore`, `test`.

**Commits:** conventional commits, see [codestyle.md](codestyle.md#commit-messages). Release notes are generated from them.

**PRs:** reference the issue (`Closes #N` or `Part of #N`). The body follows the template in the `implement-issue` skill.

**Forge operations:** through `just` only; titles are arguments, bodies are files written to `.ai/forge/` first. `just --list` shows what exists.

**Tools:** `.copier/setup.sh` installs everything on a clean Linux machine or in a cloud agent's environment; `just doctor` checks that the tools are present, recent enough ({% if platform == 'github' %}gh ≥ 2.105{% else %}fj ≥ 0.7{% endif %}) and logged in.

## Issues

**Title:** plain English and problem-focused — what is wrong or missing, not what will be done. No type prefix, no solution in the title. For research issues the title is the question.

**Body**, as sections: **Context** (what is the case today and why it is a problem), **Target** (the state after the issue is done, concrete enough to review against), **Why** (so the priority can be questioned later), **Notes** (links, open points). A research issue has **Done when** instead of Target: where the answer is decided and which document takes the result. The body is a brief, not a diary — discussion happens in comments.

{%- if platform == 'github' and github_owner_type == 'organization' %}

**Type.** This repository belongs to an organisation, so every issue has exactly one native issue type — not a `type/` label. Set it at creation by passing `type/<Name>`, with the name as the organisation defines it:

```bash
just issue-new "Title" .ai/forge/issue.md type/Bug
```

`just issue-type N TYPE` changes it later. GitHub's default types are `Bug`, `Feature` and `Task`. If the organisation defines more (research, epic, operations, …), `docs/project-management.md` lists them and what they mean.

**Labels.** `prio/high` and `prio/low` are optional; no label means normal priority. `just labels-init` creates them once per repository, and they can be passed at creation: `just issue-new TITLE FILE prio/high`.
{%- else %}

**Labels.** Every issue has exactly one `type/` label, set at creation (`just issue-new TITLE FILE type/bug`). `just labels-init` creates the set once per repository.

| Label | Use it for |
|---|---|
| `type/bug` | Behaviour that is wrong today |
| `type/feature` | Something the software should do and does not do yet, or should do differently |
| `type/task` | Work that changes no behaviour: tooling, housekeeping, setup |
| `type/docs` | Documentation only |
| `type/research` | A question to answer before work can be planned |
| `type/epic` | A parent that groups issues towards one outcome; carries no work itself |
| `type/operations` | Manual work outside the code: delivery, deployments, related systems, administration |
| `prio/high`, `prio/low` | Optional. No label means normal priority |
{%- endif %}

`area/<component>` labels are added per project when needed. There are no status labels: open/closed, the linked PR and the relations below say where an issue stands.

**Relations** are set on the forge, not described in prose:

- `just issue-blocked-by N BLOCKER` — N cannot be finished before BLOCKER. A real dependency, not "nice to have first". `just issue-unblock` removes it.
{%- if platform == 'github' %}
- `just issue-parent N PARENT` — N is a sub-issue of an epic. One parent per issue; the epic's body says what "done" means for the whole.
{%- else %}
- Forgejo has no sub-issues: an epic is blocked by each of its parts (`just issue-blocked-by EPIC PART`), and its body says what "done" means for the whole.
{%- endif %}
- Two issues that merely touch the same area: a `Related: #N` line in the body.

`just issue N` shows an issue with its comments and relations.

**Closing.** Issues close when the PR with `Closes #N` is merged. Without a PR (research, operations, superseded), close with a pointer to where the result lives: `just issue-close N FILE`.

If the repository has a `docs/project-management.md` (a shared project board with its own types and fields), that file takes precedence over this section.

## Template-owned files

This project was generated from a template and is updated from it with `just template-update`. Everything in `.copier/` belongs to the template and is replaced on update: the recipes (`recipes.just`), the setup script, the release-notes config and the recorded answers. Do not edit files there.

The root `justfile` belongs to the project. It imports the template's recipes; project recipes go there, and a recipe defined there replaces a template recipe of the same name. Mind that this also hides a template recipe added later under that name.

## Quality gates
{% if python == 'none' %}
The template brings no checks for this project's language; the project defines them as recipes in its `justfile`. The workflow relies on these names:

| Recipe | | Picked up by |
|---|---|---|
| `check` | required | CI, and the skills before a PR — everything that must pass |
| `static` | optional | the agents' Stop hook, for fast feedback (lint, typecheck) |
| `setup` | optional | CI and `.copier/setup.sh`, before `check` — install what the checks need |
| `fix` | optional | you and the agent, before committing — auto-format |

An optional recipe that does not exist is skipped; one that is added later is picked up the next time, with nothing to regenerate.
{% else %}
```bash
{% if python == 'component' %}cd {{ component_dir }} && {% endif %}uv sync --frozen    # install / sync dependencies

just fix       # ruff format + ruff check --fix — run before committing
just check     # everything CI runs
```

`just check` is `just static test`, i.e. `lint` (ruff format --check, ruff check), `typecheck` (pyright) and `test` (pytest with coverage). Each can be run alone.

| Gate | What it enforces |
|---|---|
| Format | `ruff format --check` — no auto-fix in CI |
| Lint | ruff with `select = ["ALL"]`; the ignore list is in `pyproject.toml` |
| Typecheck | pyright strict — no implicit `Any`, public functions annotated |
| Tests | pytest with branch coverage; fails below 80% |
{% endif %}
CI runs `just check` on every push and pull request.

### Pre-commit hooks
{% if hooks == 'none' %}
No pre-commit hooks are configured for this project.
{%- else %}
Install once after cloning with `pre-commit install`; run all of them with `pre-commit run --all-files`. Which hooks run is defined in `.pre-commit-config.yaml` — that file is the list.

`detect-secrets` runs without a baseline file: every finding blocks the commit. Mark a false positive on its line:

```python
EXAMPLE_KEY = "AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"  # pragma: allowlist secret
```

If a project accumulates false positives that cannot be annotated inline (generated files, fixtures full of fake keys), switch to a baseline: run `detect-secrets scan > .secrets.baseline`, commit the file, and add `args: ["--baseline", ".secrets.baseline"]` to the hook in `.pre-commit-config.yaml`.
{%- endif %}

### Definition of done

A change is ready to merge when:

- [ ] `just check` passes locally and CI is green on the PR
- [ ] New behaviour has test coverage
- [ ] `docs/` is updated where the change affects documented behaviour
- [ ] Any new architectural decision is recorded in `docs/adr/`
- [ ] The PR body says how the change was verified and what was not

## Releases

Releases are cut from `main` with `just release vX.Y.Z` ([semver](https://semver.org/)), on the human's request; the procedure is the `release` skill.

The recipe refuses to run unless it is on a clean `main` that matches `origin/main`. It generates the release notes from the conventional commits since the last tag (git-cliff, configured in `.copier/cliff.toml`) and creates the tag and the release page on {% if platform == 'github' %}GitHub{% else %}Forgejo{% endif %}. The tag triggers the CD workflow{% if is_service %}, which builds and pushes the Docker image{% endif %}.

There is no `CHANGELOG.md`: the release pages are the changelog (`just releases`), so a release needs no commit and no PR.
